# proc/refused.poke -- the ways spawn says no, and the one way it says yes to
# something that then fails.
#
# The interesting case is the last kind: spawning a program that does not
# exist SUCCEEDS. fork worked, the child ran, execve failed, and the child
# exited 127. That is POSIX, it is what every shell reports, and a broker that
# tried to hide it would have to wait for the child before answering -- which
# would make every spawn synchronous to make one error tidier.
#
# An earlier version of this fixture checked that ".." and an absolute path
# were refused as spawn paths. Neither is refused any more, for the reason in
# path.c: a literal path is the cheapest thing a brainfuck program can
# produce, and refusing one only ever cost reachability.
#
# Frames transcribed by hand from ABI.md sections 4 and 7.14 to 7.16.
#
#   hello  op 01, len 10
EMIT 01 0a 00 42 53 54 4d 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 107
#   pipe with an undefined flag bit -- INVAL 06
EMIT 0e 02 00 01 00
READ 3
#   spawn with nargv 0 -- INVAL 06. A child with no argv[0] is not a thing
#   this ABI can express.
EMIT 0f 0f 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 00 00 00 00 03 00 62 66 69
READ 3
#   AN EMPTY PATH USED TO BE REFUSED HERE, and at ABI 1.1 it stopped being a
#   refusal: it now means "the interpreter the broker was launched with"
#   (section 7.15), so the case moved to bf/proc/interp.poke, where it is a
#   success rather than a failure.
#
#   What that cost is worth writing down. This was the ONE caller that could
#   reach src/path.c's zero-length rule -- the filesystem ops refuse an empty
#   path a layer higher, in path_of -- so that rule appeared to lose its only
#   test. It did not. The substitution happens BEFORE the path check, so
#   removing the substitution makes an empty path fall through to exactly that
#   rule, and tier 11's the-interpreter-substitution row does precisely that.
#   The rule is still what makes the mutation visible; only the direction of
#   the test turned over.
#   spawn whose descriptor map names handle 0x63, which was never issued --
#   BADF 03. A program can only hand a child a descriptor it holds, and the
#   generation check means it cannot hand over one it closed.
EMIT 0f 19 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 01 01 00 00
EMIT 00 63 00 00 00
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
EMIT 03 00 62 66 69
READ 3
#   spawn "nope", which does not exist.  This SUCCEEDS: handle 4.
EMIT 0f 16 00 ff ff ff ff 00 00 00 01 00 00
EMIT 04 00 6e 6f 70 65
EMIT 04 00 6e 6f 70 65
READ 7
#   wait for it -- state 1 exited, code 127, because the child could not exec
EMIT 10 06 00 04 00 00 00 00 00
READ 7
#   wait on handle 1, which is the broker's stdin and not a process -- BADF 03,
#   from the handle's kind
EMIT 10 06 00 01 00 00 00 00 00
READ 3
#   close the process handle for the child that could not exec
EMIT 0c 04 00 04 00 00 00
READ 3
#   exit  op 02, len 1, code 0
EMIT 02 01 00 00
READ 3
